        <mxCell id="300" value="D. KEY TRADE-OFFS (choice vs named alternative — and what is given up)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1F4E79;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3180" width="800" height="24" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="301" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Kafka&lt;/b&gt; over &lt;i&gt;SQS / Kinesis&lt;/i&gt; — need replay from an arbitrary offset for reconciliation and backfill, plus key-ordered partitions.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; operational weight (or MSK cost); partition count becomes a capacity-planning decision.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Flink / Kafka Streams&lt;/b&gt; over &lt;i&gt;a hand-rolled consumer with a Redis dedupe set&lt;/i&gt; — gives transactional exactly-once into the sink.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;A Redis dedupe set is NOT durable: eviction or failover admits duplicates → overcharging, which violates a stated NFR.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; a stateful framework to operate, checkpoint storage, and slower recovery on restart.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;DynamoDB/Cassandra&lt;/b&gt; for aggregates over &lt;i&gt;Postgres&lt;/i&gt; — predictable single-digit-ms upserts at 50k/s on a known composite key.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; ad-hoc queries and joins; no cross-partition transactions; must design every access pattern up front.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Postgres&lt;/b&gt; for invoices/plans over &lt;i&gt;the same NoSQL store&lt;/i&gt; — low volume (50k rows/month), needs multi-row transactions, joins, and strict&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;correctness for money. &lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; a second datastore to operate. Worth it — this is the polyglot-persistence call.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;S3 Parquet&lt;/b&gt; for raw events over &lt;i&gt;keeping raw in DynamoDB&lt;/i&gt; — ~15 TB/period; Dynamo at that size and write rate is an order of magnitude&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;more expensive. &lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; low-latency single-event lookup; audit drill-down becomes an Athena query, seconds not milliseconds.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Store quantity, price at read time&lt;/b&gt; over &lt;i&gt;a running price total&lt;/i&gt; — two independent reasons: (1) an increment is not idempotent under&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;at-least-once, (2) tiered pricing means sum(price) ≠ price(sum), so a running price is arithmetically wrong, not just fragile.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; read cost — which is exactly why the rollup hierarchy has to exist.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hierarchical rollups&lt;/b&gt; over &lt;i&gt;minute rows only&lt;/i&gt; — 43,200 min-rows/customer/meter/period × 20 meters ≈ 864k rows per spend query.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Rollups cut it to ~115. &lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; a compaction job to operate, and a window where MIN and HOUR rows briefly disagree.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;60s cache with asOf/isEstimate&lt;/b&gt; over &lt;i&gt;a strongly-consistent read&lt;/i&gt; — the SLA already permits 3 min of staleness, so buy the read&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;amplification back. &lt;b&gt;Giving up:&lt;/b&gt; nothing real, PROVIDED the API tells the caller the number is an estimate. Hence the field.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fillColor=#FDF3F3;strokeColor=#B85450;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3208" width="1680" height="420" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <mxCell id="400" value="E. DEEP DIVES — the hard problems specific to THIS system" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;fontColor=#1F4E79;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3670" width="800" height="24" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="401" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;1. EXACTLY-ONCE UNDER AT-LEAST-ONCE (the money-correctness core)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Never do &quot;append event&quot; + &quot;increment total&quot; as two writes — a crash between them undercounts forever, and the client's retry is&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;rejected as a duplicate so it never self-heals. Eliminate the second write instead of trying to make the pair atomic.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Aggregation must be &lt;b&gt;idempotent&lt;/b&gt;, not merely at-least-once: the window's output is a function of the set of eventIds in it, so&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;replaying the same window produces the same row. Use Flink checkpointing + a transactional sink, or version the bucket row&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;with the Kafka offset watermark and reject stale writes with a conditional update.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;2. THE SCALE BREAK — read amplification (this is the one Aayush's final design hits)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;1-min buckets = 1,440/day × 30 = &lt;b&gt;43,200 rows per customer per meter per period&lt;/b&gt;; × 20 meters ≈ &lt;b&gt;864,000 rows&lt;/b&gt; summed on&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;every current-spend page load. That does not meet an interactive SLA for the top 1% of tenants.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; MIN → HOUR → DAY rollups. A spend query reads ~30 DAY + ~24 HOUR + ~60 MIN ≈ &lt;b&gt;115 rows&lt;/b&gt;. 60s Redis cache on top.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;3. HOT TENANTS (top 1% = 50% of volume — this was stated in the prompt)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Partitioning by customerId alone puts one tenant's ~25k events/s on a single partition → guaranteed hot partition and&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;throttling. &lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; composite key &lt;b&gt;customerId#shard&lt;/b&gt;, shard ∈ [0,N), with N per-tenant and adaptive (1 for the long tail,&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;64+ for the whales). Reads scatter-gather across N shards; N is stored on the Subscription so it can be raised online.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;4. LATE &amp;amp; OUT-OF-ORDER EVENTS (the hardest real billing problem)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;eventTime ≠ ingestTime. An event timestamped inside a period can arrive after that period was invoiced.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;Policy:&lt;/b&gt; period goes OPEN → CLOSING (24h grace, no invoice yet) → FINALIZED. Events arriving in CLOSING land normally.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Events arriving after FINALIZED become an &lt;b&gt;Adjustment&lt;/b&gt; line on the NEXT invoice — never a silent edit of a sent invoice.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Watermarks in the stream job bound how late is &quot;too late&quot;; anything beyond goes to the DLQ with a business alert.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;5. ANNIVERSARY-ALIGNED PERIOD CLOSING&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Boundaries land on all 28+ days of the month, so finalization is a continuous background process, not a month-end batch.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;The scheduler must be sharded, idempotent (exactly one invoice per customer+period, enforced by a unique constraint),&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;resumable after a crash, and timezone-aware. &quot;Runs twice&quot; must be a no-op, not a double bill.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;6. TIERED PRICING&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Price is a step function of cumulative period quantity, so it can only be evaluated on the TOTAL — never incrementally.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Plans are &lt;b&gt;versioned&lt;/b&gt; and invoices store planVersion + a tierBreakdown per line item, so any invoice is reproducible and&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;defensible in a dispute months later.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;7. OPERABILITY — &quot;how do I know the bill is wrong BEFORE the customer tells me?&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;• Consumer lag alert vs the 3-min freshness SLA; auto-scale consumers on lag, and surface staleness via &lt;b&gt;asOf&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;• &lt;b&gt;Daily reconciliation:&lt;/b&gt; count raw events in S3 vs sum of aggregate rows, per customer per meter. Any drift pages.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;• &lt;b&gt;Invoice-delta alert:&lt;/b&gt; this bill is &amp;gt;10× the trailing average → hold in DRAFT for human review before it is sent&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;• DLQ depth alert, with a documented replay path; zero-usage alert (a silent emitter is undercounting = lost revenue)&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;• Full replay from S3 must be able to rebuild every aggregate from scratch — this is the ultimate correctness backstop&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;b&gt;8. COST AT SCALE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;500M events/day × 1 KB = ~500 GB/day → ~15 TB/period raw (S3 ≈ $350/mo, vs multiple $10k/mo in DynamoDB).&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Aggregates are ~43k rows/customer/meter/period pre-rollup; post-rollup the retained set is orders of magnitude smaller.&lt;br/&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Raw retention 90 days (dispute window), aggregates 7 years (financial record).&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=6;fillColor=#FDF3F3;strokeColor=#B85450;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="3698" width="1680" height="800" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
